<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Sit Down.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Identify characters in a picture book. reading_2: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures. phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify and name characters in familiar picture books by responding to a prompt (e.g., pointing to 'Alice' when hearing the instruction as the teacher read the story). Can match spoken words to their written form with picture support during shared reading activities (e.g., hearing 'cat' and pointing to the written word 'cat' with a picture of a cat). Can identify the names of letters (e.g., A, B, C) at the beginning of familiar words by pointing or gesturing (e.g., pointing to the letter 'B' or clapping when hearing or seeing the letter 'B' in a familiar word, like 'B' in 'big').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" style game using known vocabulary and actions related to classroom routines. "Simon says: sit down!" (students sit), "Simon says: pick up the pen!" (students pretend to pick up a pen). The teacher reinforces "Please, sit down" and "Please, pick up." This activates physical engagement and prior vocabulary from Lesson 1, connecting to the theme "First, I Sit Down." The teacher also shows large flashcards of letters A, B, C, D, E and says their names clearly, having students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture card of a `chair` and says "Chair. This is a chair." Then holds up a word card with "chair" written on it and points to the word while saying "chair" again. The teacher models the pronunciation of /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down' through exaggerated repetition: "P-p-pick up!" "D-d-down!" using clear, slow speech and gestures. For letters, the teacher points to a large letter 'A' card and says "A, A for apple."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a picture of a chair and a desk) "Is this a chair or a desk?" (Students point to `chair`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word 'sit' and acting it out) "What am I doing?" (Students might say "Sit!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the letter 'B') "What letter is this?" (Students point or say 'B').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">[Date]<br>5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Routines".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book about a classroom routine, e.g., "Our Busy School Day" or "First, I Sit Down." (a simplified narrative excerpt). The book should feature familiar characters (e.g., a boy named Ben and a girl named Mia) performing classroom routines and use target vocabulary like `sit`, `chair`, `desk`, `pen`, `paper`, `notebook`, `bag`, `pick up`, `put down`. The teacher uses large text or a projected version of the book. The genre is a simple narrative picture book, register is informal/child-friendly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Identify characters in a picture book; Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures. Phonemic Awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to each word as they read.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> (After reading the first page with a character named 'Ben' sitting) "Who is this?" (Teacher points to Ben. Students point to Ben in the book/picture).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching (Word to Picture):</b> (Teacher reads "Ben sits on the chair." and points to 'chair' in the sentence) "Show me 'chair'!" (Students point to the picture of the chair).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching (Spoken to Written word):</b> (Teacher says "pen" while pointing to the word 'pen' in the book) "Find 'pen'!" (Students point to the word 'pen' on the page).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Letter Recognition:</b> (Teacher points to the word 'bag' in the story) "What letter is this at the beginning of 'bag'?" (Teacher points to 'B'. Students identify 'B'). "Great, 'B' for 'bag'!"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">[Date]<br>10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading words with picture support; Identifying characters; Recognizing letters (A, B, C, D, E). Grammar: Present simple ('This is a...'), Imperatives ('Pick it up.'). Vocabulary: `chair`, `desk`, `bag`, `pen`, `paper`, `notebook`, `sit`, `pick up`, `put down`, `on`, `up`, `down`, `Please`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Picture-Word Matching (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes picture cards (e.g., `chair`, `desk`, `pen`, `bag`) and corresponding word cards to each student or small groups. Students are instructed to "Match the picture to the word!" The teacher monitors and helps students align the word 'chair' with the picture of a chair, 'pen' with the picture of a pen. The teacher reinforces the /p/ sound in 'pen' and /p/ in 'paper'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Read and Point (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher displays large sentence strips with simple sentences from the story, e.g., "First, I sit down on my chair." and "Please, pick up your pen." Each sentence strip has small pictures above the key vocabulary words for support. The teacher reads each sentence aloud slowly, and students point to the words as they hear them. For example, "First, I `sit` `down` on my `chair`." (Students point to `sit`, `down`, `chair`). The teacher pays attention to the /d/ sound in 'down'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Letter Hunt (Individual/Peer Check):</b> The teacher gives each student a worksheet with 5-6 simple words (e.g., `apple`, `ball`, `cat`, `desk`, `eraser`) and asks them to circle the first letter of each word. The letters A, B, C, D, E are clearly visible as the beginning of these words. Students then compare their circled letters with a partner, saying the letter names aloud, e.g., "A for apple!" "B for ball!" The teacher walks around, providing individual support and checking recognition.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">[Date]<br>15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Routines".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Shared Reading with Action and Pointing. Students engage in a collaborative "Routine Reading" activity, using simplified routine sequences with picture and word support. The task focuses on applying their reading skills to understand and follow classroom routines based on the theme "First, I Sit Down."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher projects or displays a large poster with a 3-step routine:
                            <br><b>Step 1:</b> Sit down. (Picture of child sitting on a `chair`)
                            <br><b>Step 2:</b> Take the pen. (Picture of child with a `pen` and `paper`)
                            <br><b>Step 3:</b> Put the bag on the desk. (Picture of `bag` on `desk`)
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher first reads the entire routine aloud, pointing to the words and pictures for each step.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are then divided into small groups or work as a whole class. The teacher re-reads each step, and students are asked to "read" by pointing to the correct words as they hear them AND performing the action. For example, the teacher says "Step 1: Sit down." Students point to 'Sit down' on the poster and then sit down in their chairs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides sentence frames "This is a [chair/pen]" or "Please, [sit/pick up]" to guide students as they respond to the text and pictures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For an additional challenge, the teacher points to a word like 'pen' or 'chair' on the poster and asks, "What is this?" or "What letter is this?" (pointing to the initial letter 'P' in 'Pen' or 'C' in 'Chair').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">[Date]<br>10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Routine Reading" activity and "Letter Hunt." The teacher notes which students can successfully point to characters, match spoken words to written words with picture support, and recognize initial letters (A, B, C, D, E).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the following actions based on teacher prompts while looking at the routine poster or word cards:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Point to the character (e.g., 'Ben') in the storybook when prompted by the teacher.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Point to the word 'chair' on a word card or in the routine poster when the teacher says "chair."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Point to the letter 'D' on an alphabet chart or in the word 'desk' on a word card.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identification of Characters:</b> Can point to familiar characters in the picture book when named (e.g., 'Ben', 'Mia').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Spoken to Written Word:</b> Can point to 3-4 target vocabulary words (`sit`, `chair`, `pen`, `bag`) on cards or a poster when spoken.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Letter Recognition:</b> Can identify 2-3 target letters (A, B, C, D, E) when prompted.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a `chair` and the word 'chair') "Can you find the word 'chair'?" (Students point).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up letter 'A') "What letter is this?" (Students say or point to 'A').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">[Date]<br>5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you like to read today?" (e.g., `pen`, `sit`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a letter you learned today?" (Students point to A, B, C, D, or E).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 15px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson for Kinder students? The shared reading with large picture books and word cards seemed highly engaging, and students responded well to pointing tasks. Identifying initial letters with pictures also worked effectively.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still confuse similar-looking words. For the next lesson, I might need more individual practice with word-picture matching, perhaps using laminated cards they can manipulate. I also need to ensure consistent reinforcement of the letters A-E.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and recognizing words and letters. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students verbally describe and interact using these same routine words and actions. They will use the vocabulary they just read to speak about their classroom routines, using imperatives and present simple structures in pairs.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">[Date]<br>3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>